asia's largest gallery is set to open in  hong kong this week the towering  90-meter structure has been in the works  for about a decade and features a  billion-dollar collection but the m-plus  museum is not without controversy as it  opens under a new restrictive political  climate china imposed a national  security law on hong kong in june which  critics say is an attack on the freedom  of speech and meant to crush dissent  anyone can be charged under a vague and  broad law for undermining beijing's  authority even artists  hong kong native casey wong is well  known for his outspoken political art  use of public space and even elaborate  displays at street protests  this used to be where he staged his  performances and artworks  several years ago two of his pieces were  purchased by m plus hong kong's new  contemporary art museum like this  floating house which casey wong paddled  on the harbor questioning the city's  expensive tiny flats  my artwork was critical of the injustice  of society  i felt that m plus was professional in  picking the collections they were  interested in those reflecting local  stories and histories  m-plus is a highlight of hong kong's  newly built cultural district a  government-funded landmark project that  has taken over two decades to realize  with its collection of over 8 400  artworks the museum positions itself as  a top regional art hub and aims to rival  western heavyweights such as london's  tate modern and new york's museum of  modern art  what draws particular attention is its  arguably largest and finest collection  of contemporary chinese art in the world  thanks to swiss collector uli sig who  donated and sold over 1 500 pieces to m  plus including some created by chinese  distant artist ai weiwei  a famous picture of him raising the  middle finger at beijing's tiananmen  square in the 1990s was censored on m  plus museum website that's after  pro-beijing politicians and media  contended it could spread hatred against  china  but pictures from the same series in  front of the white house and the swiss  parliament remain  authorities have confirmed that the  censored picture won't be shown at the  opening exhibition  and vowed to work with the national  security unit if any artwork violates  the beijing-imposed security law  it seems to me it's a series of  systematic attacks against the art and  cultural sector  the political crackdown from beijing is  becoming more tangible  the museum authorities have let us down  by giving up its autonomy and betraying  the artistic profession  why should an artwork be judged by the  police  the much anticipated museum kicks off a  new era for hong kong's cultural sphere  at a time when its future is clouded by  beijing's tightening grip on freedom of  speech  casey wong won't have the chance to  witness his artwork being displayed in m  plus that's because he left hong kong  for taiwan over the summer  a new platform doesn't necessarily  facilitate art development if it helps  exploit freedom of art expression  self-censorship is getting more common  among artists who tend to make their  works more abstract but i believe that  the greater the limitation  the greater the creativity that will  evolve  let's say goodbye  with a smile dear  and so he said goodbye to hong kong  his last performance was a farewell  music video dedicated to the city  filmed before his departure to taiwan  in it he sings vera lynn's will meet  again  asking hong kong to keep smiling till  the blue skies drive the dark clouds  away  [Music]  we're now joined by dw correspondent  phoebe kong phoebe long delays it's over  budget but the m-plus museum is finally  open what are some of the highlights  well emplous is a star project of hong  kong's west kowloon cultural district  it was a newly built  landmark like pretty much located at the  heart of hong kong in downtown  of the city so today we are also invited  to the media preview like um having a  fresh look at the new galleries that are  there those artworks that contains like  many of the paintings drawings and most  of them were created by artists from  mainland china  hong kong of course across asia and uh  some of the pieces were also created by  um western artists but um those not the  majorities of the art pieces so it  features very much like put the focus on  the asian contemporary art especially  the mainland chinese artwork in recent  decades um so we also found some like  artwork like um echoing some of the  chinese political and social incidents  in recent decades  now we saw in the report that part of an  iway way series was removed from the  museum website and won't be part of the  exhibit can you talk to us about what  happened there  so earlier this year in hong kong  some of the probation lawmakers and also  state-owned media outlets criticized  some of the art collections of the  empress museum of insulting  the china and also like inciting hatred  against the chinese government and also  china um so they uh criticized that  those artwork may violate the national  security imposed by beijing in hong kong  last june so um after that like it  triggered a huge controversy among the  artistic sphere and also like among  society it triggered huge debate on like  how to deal with the new artwork under  the national security law so after that  um like um one of the uh  pieces that i put on the spotlight is ai  wayways famous photograph showing his  middle finger to beijing's tenement  square  so um that photo was particularly  removed from empress museum official  website without specific reasoning so  today at the media preview the head of  the cultural district reiterated again  that  those artwork including highways photos  are now under review that  is not yet known that whether it will be  displayed in the future but  surely today in the preview and at the  opening exhibition those photographs are  absent from the galleries right now  phoebe hong kong has been burgeoning as  a hub for arts and culture in the region  how much does china's national security  law undermine and hurt that label  well the sweeping national security law  has been imposed in hong kong since last  last june it has been almost one and a  half year and here it has created um a  very huge impact among like almost all  kinds of sectors in hong kong so it's  not only affecting the artists we have  heard many like um voices from the  artistic sectors that they will be like  self-censoring their works because of  the pressure and also the pressure from  the commercial sectors and the financial  pressure they're facing so um they're  pretty like pessimistic to the future of  the like art and culture development in  hong kong  dw correspondent phoebe kong reporting  for us thank you phoebe
 

